 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\ntyping.NamedTuple inference broken with incorrect argument validation\n\n#### Description\n\nWhen using `typing.NamedTuple` with the standard two-argument form, astroid fails to properly infer the namedtuple class and instead returns a generic `typing.NamedTuple` instance.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nimport astroid\nfrom typing import NamedTuple\n\n# This should create a proper namedtuple class but fails\ncode = '''\nfrom typing import NamedTuple\nPoint = NamedTuple(\"Point\", [(\"x\", int), (\"y\", int)])\n'''\n\nmodule = astroid.parse(code)\npoint_class = module.body[1].value\ninferred = next(point_class.infer())\n\nprint(f\"Expected: Point class\")\nprint(f\"Actual: {inferred}\")\nprint(f\"Type: {type(inferred)}\")\n```\n\nThe inference should create a proper namedtuple class with the specified fields, but instead returns a generic `typing.NamedTuple` instance.\n\nThis also affects inheritance scenarios:\n\n```python\ncode = '''\nfrom typing import NamedTuple\n\nclass MyPoint(NamedTuple(\"Point\", [(\"x\", int), (\"y\", int)])):\n    pass\n'''\n```\n\nThe base class inference fails, preventing proper ancestor resolution.\n</issue>\n",
